      "contentType": "text",
      "media": [],
      "hasMediaInOriginal": false,
      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "When the SA80A3 entered service in 2018, it marked a major upgrade for the British Army, but development continued after its introduction. Following an initial £15 million modernisation contract, the rifle was refined in response to soldier feedback. By 2020, the most noticeable change was the replacement of Heckler & Koch's HKey attachment system with the industry-standard M-LOK handguard.\n\nThe switch to M-LOK was driven by testing and user feedback. The Infantry Trials and Development Unit worked with HK UK to develop and trial 3D-printed prototypes, allowing design changes to be tested and refined quickly. After acquiring its own 3D printer, the unit was able to carry out rapid in-house development. The redesigned handguard improved accessory compatibility with M-LOK slots, strengthened the lower rail for the standard-issue Grip Pod foregrip, reduced heat transfer around the gas block, improved the safety guard's ergonomics, and introduced a dark green finish in place of the earlier phosphor bronze.\n\nThe updated handguard was fitted to newly built rifles, while existing SA80A3s were retrofitted as they returned for maintenance. The programme also maintained domestic manufacturing, with every SA80A3 assembled in the UK by British workers, supporting national industrial capability alongside the rifle's continued development.\n\n(Part 2/2)\n\n@medmannews",
      "links": [
        {
          "label": "@medmannews",
          "url": "https://t.me/medmannews"
        }
      ],
